WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.720
All right, my friends.

00:04.720 --> 00:06.560
So we're gonna start with a secret.

00:06.600 --> 00:10.720
A little trick that I usually do by analyzing any data sets.

00:10.760 --> 00:13.080
So let's start with little coffee before we start.

00:14.040 --> 00:15.480
Um, this is really hot.

00:15.640 --> 00:20.640
Okay, so the secret states, as I'm looking to any data sets in, any projects, I see the data always

00:20.640 --> 00:23.800
divided between dimensions and measures.

00:25.880 --> 00:26.680
What truth?

00:27.200 --> 00:29.760
You take the blue pill, you take the red pill.

00:30.120 --> 00:32.440
All I'm offering is the truth, nothing more.

00:34.240 --> 00:40.960
If you see your data like me as dimensions and measures, you can generate like endless amount of insights

00:40.960 --> 00:43.040
from any projects, from any data sets.

00:43.080 --> 00:48.280
And you will find me through the projects that I'm always speaking about measures and dimensions.

00:48.280 --> 00:50.080
So I'm going to show you how I usually do it.

00:50.080 --> 00:53.240
So now usually by looking to any data set in any project.

00:53.240 --> 00:55.400
So you have like multiple columns and rows.

00:55.440 --> 01:00.960
Here I see the data always splitted into two categories, either a dimension or a measure.

01:01.000 --> 01:05.480
And now of course the question is here is my column a dimension or a measure?

01:05.520 --> 01:10.690
Well, in order to assign it to one of those categories, you have to ask the first question.

01:10.690 --> 01:12.050
Is it a numeric value?

01:12.050 --> 01:17.530
If it's not so you have like string or date or any other data type, then it is a dimension.

01:17.530 --> 01:21.770
And if it is yes in numeric then you have to ask the second question.

01:21.810 --> 01:24.090
Does it make sense to aggregate it?

01:24.090 --> 01:30.490
So if the answer for both questions is yes, it is numeric and it makes sense to aggregate it, then

01:30.490 --> 01:31.290
it is a measure.

01:31.330 --> 01:32.930
Otherwise it is a dimension.

01:32.970 --> 01:35.090
Now let's practice and have some examples.

01:35.090 --> 01:40.410
So now by looking to the values of the column category you can see all the values are characters.

01:40.410 --> 01:41.570
So it is not numeric.

01:41.610 --> 01:44.050
That means this column is a dimension.

01:44.050 --> 01:45.130
So it is very simple.

01:45.130 --> 01:46.250
Let's take another column.

01:46.250 --> 01:47.690
We have the sales amount.

01:47.690 --> 01:49.810
So now as you can see the values are numeric.

01:49.810 --> 01:52.690
And as well it makes sense to aggregate those values.

01:52.690 --> 01:56.130
We can get the total sales or the average sales and so on.

01:56.130 --> 01:57.770
So it fulfills both of the conditions.

01:57.770 --> 02:00.410
It is numeric and it makes sense to aggregate it.

02:00.410 --> 02:03.090
That's why we say sales is a measure.

02:03.130 --> 02:07.770
Now if you are checking the values of the product name, you can see that all of them are characters

02:07.770 --> 02:08.410
and names.

02:08.410 --> 02:09.490
So it is not numeric.

02:09.490 --> 02:11.930
That means the product is dimension.

02:11.930 --> 02:14.130
Moving on to the next one, we have the quantity.

02:14.130 --> 02:17.900
The values are numeric and as well it makes sense to aggregate.

02:17.900 --> 02:20.700
It can summarize all those values to have their total quantity.

02:20.700 --> 02:22.220
So quantity is a measure.

02:22.260 --> 02:26.540
Now if you are looking to the values of the birth dates you can see this is a date information.

02:26.540 --> 02:27.700
It is not numeric.

02:27.700 --> 02:29.860
So that means it is a dimension right.

02:29.900 --> 02:36.940
But if you calculate the age from the birth dates, age of the customer can be in numeric and it makes

02:36.940 --> 02:37.980
sense to aggregate it.

02:37.980 --> 02:40.780
For example finding the average age of customers.

02:40.780 --> 02:46.660
So if we derive a numeric value from a dimension then we can use it as a measure.

02:46.660 --> 02:48.180
So age is a measure.

02:48.180 --> 02:50.180
And now we come to something really tricky.

02:50.180 --> 02:51.500
This is the id.

02:51.500 --> 02:56.580
So for example if you are checking the customer ID you can see all those values are numeric.

02:56.620 --> 02:58.540
So the first condition is fulfilled.

02:58.580 --> 03:03.180
Now the very important question does it make sense to aggregate the IDs.

03:03.180 --> 03:06.020
Well those IDs are unique identifier for a customer.

03:06.020 --> 03:09.740
And if you find like the average of that it is not like helpful.

03:09.740 --> 03:10.140
Right?

03:10.140 --> 03:16.740
I cannot think of one use case of aggregating the customer ID like having the average of all those IDs

03:16.740 --> 03:20.940
or summarizing the IDs, so it makes no sense to aggregate it.

03:20.940 --> 03:26.190
That's why we can consider the ID of a customer as a dimension, not as a measure.

03:26.190 --> 03:27.830
So as you can see it is very simple.

03:27.830 --> 03:31.710
If it is numeric and it makes sense to aggregate, then it is measure.

03:31.750 --> 03:33.510
Otherwise it is a dimension.

03:33.510 --> 03:36.710
And this is the foundations of any data analytics.

03:36.710 --> 03:43.230
If you see your data as dimensions and measures, you can generate a lot of use cases and insights from

03:43.230 --> 03:44.230
your datasets.

03:44.270 --> 03:48.390
Now I totally understand if you are still confused about dimensions and measures, and you might be

03:48.430 --> 03:51.150
asking why do I need measures and dimensions?

03:51.230 --> 03:56.190
Well, if you are doing any type of data analysis or you are exploring any datasets, you will be end

03:56.230 --> 04:01.670
up always like grouping up the data by something like you are grouping the data by countries or grouping

04:01.670 --> 04:05.110
the data by, for example, products or categories.

04:05.110 --> 04:08.070
So we need dimensions to group up our data.

04:08.070 --> 04:11.870
And in the other side you will be asking questions like how much, how many?

04:11.910 --> 04:13.590
What is the total of something.

04:13.590 --> 04:16.750
So you always need to aggregate or calculate something right.

04:16.790 --> 04:18.470
And for that you need the measure.

04:18.470 --> 04:22.590
So we need the measures in order to answer the question how many and how much.

04:22.590 --> 04:26.990
And we need the dimensions in order to group up the data by something.

04:26.990 --> 04:31.710
So that's why almost in any type of data analysis you need dimensions and measures.

04:31.710 --> 04:34.750
And this is going to be more clear as we progress in the projects.
